'use strict';
$('experiment-content').innerHTML=`
${ExperimentGuide.render()}
<div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT THE EXPERIMENT FOUND</p><h2>Information mattered most before families enrolled.</h2><p>Estimated effects for families not yet enrolled when the intervention took place:</p></div>
<div class="outcome-grid"><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Higher-quality schools</h3><strong>+0.099 SD</strong><p>Value added of the school chosen.</p><small>Standard error: 0.037</small></article><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Willingness to travel</h3><strong>+0.198 km</strong><p>Distance to the chosen school.</p><small>Standard error: 0.073</small></article><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Different school choices</h3><strong>+12.3 pp</strong><p>Probability of choosing a fee-charging school.</p><small>Standard error: 4.0 percentage points</small></article></div>
<p class="fine-print">Assignment was at the preschool level; 133 of the 143 assigned preschools participated. The analysis pools the information treatment. Enrollment timing is measured retrospectively, and full-sample effects are weaker. Source: manuscript, experiment setting and reduced-form school-choice table.</p>
<details class="methods"><summary>What happened to children’s learning?</summary><p>Among the not-yet-enrolled subgroup, estimated fourth-grade test-score effects are +0.241 SD in language (SE 0.071) and +0.209 SD in mathematics (SE 0.068). These later outcomes use smaller, potentially selected test-taking samples. They are separate experimental outcomes and are not used to estimate the structural model.</p><a href="paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the outcome analysis ↗</a></details>
<div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH EXPLAINED</p><h2>Hear the question behind the study.</h2></div><div class="story-grid"><article class="story-card"><div class="video-embed" id="research-video"><button class="video-load" id="load-video" type="button"><span class="play-symbol" aria-hidden="true">▶</span><strong>Watch Francisco Gallego explain the research</strong><span>Instituto de Economía UC · Spanish</span></button></div><div class="video-caption"><span>Research explainer · not the treatment video</span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bxAtTqQ-FVM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube ↗</a></div></article><div class="story-pad"><h2>From individual choices to a whole market</h2><p>A small experiment tells us how families respond when information changes. Scaling up raises a new question: what happens when many families compete for the same schools, and schools have a reason to improve?</p><p>The experiment took place through Fundación Integra parent meetings in urban Valparaíso, Biobío, and Santiago, from August to December 2010.</p><p><a href="https://economia.uc.cl/2020/07/28/la-uc-participa-en-desarrollo-de-herramienta-virtual-para-comparar-colegios-con-informacion-personalizada/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UC’s research announcement ↗</a></p></div></div>
<div class="end-action"><div><h2>How do these findings enter the model?</h2><p>Follow the observed change in choice into the policy projections.</p></div><a class="button-link" href="#model">Evidence to model →</a></div>`;
$('model-content').innerHTML=`
<div class="pipeline"><article><span class="step-num">01 / BASELINE DEMAND</span><h2>Estimate baseline school demand</h2><p>The demand model uses observed first-grade school choices across 53 urban markets, accounting for quality, out-of-pocket fees, distance, and heterogeneity across families.</p></article><article><span class="step-num">02 / EXPERIMENTAL EVIDENCE</span><h2>Identify treatment effects on school choice</h2><p>Random assignment identifies treatment effects on school-choice outcomes. The structural estimation sample comprises 815 not-yet-enrolled families in six overlapping markets.</p></article><article><span class="step-num">03 / STRUCTURAL ESTIMATION</span><h2>Estimate treatment-induced demand shifts</h2><p>Treatment parameters are estimated separately by SEP status, matching changes in chosen-school quality, prices, distance, voucher-school attendance, and residual school appeal.</p></article><article><span class="step-num">04 / COUNTERFACTUAL ANALYSIS</span><h2>Evaluate equilibrium policy effects</h2><p>Apply the estimated shifts to the 2011 population, combine treated and untreated demand, impose capacity constraints, and solve for private schools’ quality choices.</p></article></div>
<div class="equation-panel"><div><p class="eyebrow" style="color:#a9bdff">TREATMENT EFFECTS IN THE DEMAND MODEL</p><h2>Incorporating the experimental treatment into school demand</h2><p>The model allows both attribute-specific shifts and a rescaling of choice behavior within each SEP group. These effective shifts can reflect information, salience, or preferences; those channels are not separately identified.</p></div><div><div class="equation">${PaperMath.block('treatment')}</div><div class="equation-key"><span>${PaperMath.inline('q_j')}: school quality</span><span>${PaperMath.inline(String.raw`\text{op}_{ij}`)}: family payment</span><span>${PaperMath.inline('d_{ij}')}: driving distance</span><span>${PaperMath.inline(String.raw`\kappa_k`)}: scale of baseline utility</span><span>${PaperMath.inline(String.raw`\psi_k^q,\ \psi_k^{op},\ \psi_k^d`)}: attribute shifts</span><span>${PaperMath.inline('C')}: untreated · ${PaperMath.inline('T')}: treated</span></div><p class="equation-context">${PaperMath.inline('i')} identifies a family, ${PaperMath.inline('j')} a school, and ${PaperMath.inline('k')} a family type. Treatment coefficients vary by SEP group.</p><p class="equation-source"><a href="paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Treatment utility in the paper’s school-choice model ↗</a></p></div></div>
<div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">EQUILIBRIUM CHANNELS</p><h2>Decomposing policy effects: demand, capacity, and school responses</h2><p>The counterfactual analysis introduces capacity constraints and endogenous school quality sequentially to assess their implications for policy effects.</p></div>
<div class="outcome-grid"><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Demand response</h3><strong>+0.122 SD</strong><p>Choice responses with fixed school quality and prices, abstracting from capacity constraints.</p></article><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Capacity-constrained allocation</h3><strong>+0.062 SD</strong><p>Existing seat capacities are imposed, with deferred acceptance allocating places when demand exceeds supply.</p></article><article class="outcome-card"><h3>Endogenous school quality</h3><strong>+0.166 SD</strong><p>Private schools adjust quality in response to demand and competition, potentially offsetting congestion effects.</p></article></div><p class="fine-print">Reported all-family effects under full take-up among eligible families. Each number is relative to the no-intervention baseline. Units are school value-added SD, not predicted child test-score gains.</p>
<div class="compare-grid"><article><h3>Experimental inputs</h3><ul><li>The intervention-induced shift in families’ school choices.</li><li>Differences in responses across SEP and non-SEP families.</li><li>Uncertainty in the estimated treatment parameters.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Structural assumptions and mechanisms</h3><ul><li>Transporting those shifts to the scale-up population.</li><li>How seat constraints and assignment affect who attends each school.</li><li>How costs and competition shape private schools’ quality choices.</li></ul></article></div>
<details class="methods"><summary>Identification, assumptions, and interpretation</summary><p>The main equilibrium keeps tuition, school capacities, public-school quality, school entry, and school exit fixed. Private schools choose quality. The public-school response scenario is a mechanical sensitivity, not an independently estimated public-school behavioral model.</p><p>The experiment bundles a motivational video with personalized information, so it does not isolate the video’s effect or cleanly distinguish better information from changed preferences. The estimated common scale parameter κ is not significantly different from one.</p><p>The RCT’s child test-score effects do not enter structural estimation. Counterfactual outcomes measure the expected value added of the school attended. Reported inference propagates RCT treatment-parameter uncertainty, conditional on the remaining model inputs and assumptions.</p><p><a href="paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the model and estimation sections ↗</a> · <a href="appendix.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the online appendix ↗</a></p></details>
<div class="end-action"><div><h2>Policy spillovers across family groups</h2><p>Examine model-projected effects on nonrecipient families under SEP-targeted information.</p></div><button class="button-link" id="try-spillovers" type="button">Examine the spillover counterfactual →</button></div>`;
